---
import '@trieur/core/trieur.css';
import DemoNav from '../../components/DemoNav.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ShortcutsDialog from '../../components/ShortcutsDialog.astro';
---

<Base title="Demo — throw, don't drop">
  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top:44px">
    <DemoNav />
    <h1 style="margin:0 0 8px">Throw, don't drop <span class="tag" style="vertical-align:middle">experimental</span></h1>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);max-width:70ch;margin:0 0 26px">
      A drop asks you to be accurate about a <em>coordinate</em>: let go over the right cell, or the card is filed in
      the wrong folder. A throw asks you to be accurate about a <em>direction</em>, which the hand is far better at. The
      card keeps the speed it had when you released it, travels a little further on its own, and lands where that
      carries it — so a distant zone no longer has to be reached, and two neighbouring cells no longer share a border
      you have to hit.
    </p>

    <div class="grid" style="margin-bottom:26px">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Reach</h3>
        <p>
          How many milliseconds of travel the release is worth. At 170&nbsp;ms a flick of 2&nbsp;px/ms carries the card
          340&nbsp;px past your finger — the far side of the stage, from a gesture that never left the middle.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Floor</h3>
        <p>
          Below this speed a release is still an ordinary drop. It is what keeps a careful, slow drag meaning exactly
          what it looks like: the zone under the card, not the one it was heading towards.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The vector</h3>
        <p>
          The dashed line is the throw and the disc is where it lands; the region under the disc is the one that lights
          up. Turn it off in the settings once the numbers feel right — it is a tuning instrument, not a feature.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo demo-side">
      <div id="sorter"></div>
      <div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Last release</h3>
          <dl id="read" class="kv">
            <div><dt>speed</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
            <div><dt>carried</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
            <div><dt>verdict</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
            <div><dt>filed into</dt><dd>—</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Why a mosaic</h3>
          <p style="margin:0;color:var(--dim)">
            The zones here are deliberately small and adjacent — the case where dropping is a coin toss. Try the same
            gesture with <b>throw</b> off in the settings: the same flick lands in whichever cell your finger happened
            to be over when you let go.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:60px">
      <h2>Turning it on</h2>
      <pre><code>{`new Deck(el, {
  items, zones,
  layout: 'voronoi',
  flick: true,        // throw instead of drop
  flickMs: 170,       // how far ahead the release is projected
  flickMin: 0.25,     // px/ms below which it is an ordinary drop
  flickDebug: false,  // draw the vector while tuning
});`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        It is marked experimental because the right <code>flickMs</code> depends on your zones: a dock of six columns
        wants little of it, a mosaic of twenty cells wants a lot. Velocity is sampled on the raw pointer moves rather
        than the throttled ones — a flick is over in three frames, and averaging it across a frame boundary flattens
        precisely the peak that made it a flick — then smoothed, and zeroed when the finger rests before letting go: a
        gesture that stopped has thrown nothing, however fast it arrived.
      </p>
      <p>
        The projection is capped at the stage diagonal. Past the edge, every extra pixel of reach aims at the same zone
        anyway, and an uncapped throw off a fast trackpad lands in another postcode.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <ShortcutsDialog />
</Base>

<script>
  import { Deck } from '@trieur/core';
  import { createRecommender } from '@trieur/learn';
  import { meta, shuffled, zones, type Link } from '../../data/links';
  import { renderCard } from '../../scripts/card';
  import { deckGui } from '../../scripts/gui';
  import { shortcutsButton } from '../../scripts/shortcuts';

  const $ = (s: string) => document.querySelector(s)!;
  const cells = $('#read').querySelectorAll('dd');

  const deck = new Deck<Link>($('#sorter') as HTMLElement, {
    items: shuffled(24),
    zones,
    meta,
    renderCard,
    layout: 'voronoi',
    flick: true,
    flickDebug: true,
    advisor: createRecommender({ key: 'demo-flick' }),
    onSort: (_item, zone) => {
      cells[3]!.textContent = zone.id;
    },
  });

  // the deck reports every release, thrown or dropped, so the panel can show the difference
  deck.root.addEventListener('trieur:release', (e) => {
    const { speed, carried, thrown } = (e as CustomEvent).detail;
    cells[0]!.textContent = `${speed.toFixed(2)} px/ms`;
    cells[1]!.textContent = thrown ? `${Math.round(carried)} px` : '—';
    cells[2]!.textContent = thrown ? 'throw' : 'drop';
  });

  deckGui(deck, { flick: true });
  shortcutsButton(deck);
  deck.focus();
</script>
